<body>
<h1>3. Results</h1>
<h2>3.1. Diagnostic Performance and Threshold Optimization</h2>
<p>The diagnostic efficacy of the evaluated 3D deep learning architectures was strictly assessed on a completely independent external test cohort (N=37) to prevent data leakage and ensure true generalization. We evaluated four distinct paradigms: feature-extraction via linear probing (SwinUNETR-LP), self-supervised pre-training (MAE-TinyTransformer3D), attention-augmented full fine-tuning (Attention-SwinUNETR), and hierarchical multi-scale transformers (SegFormer3D-MSCA). The primary endpoint metrics—Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC), Sensitivity, Specificity, and Predictive Values—are presented with 95% Bootstrap Confidence Intervals (CIs) in <strong>Table 1</strong>.</p>
<p>The <strong>SwinUNETR-LP</strong> architecture demonstrated superior discriminative capacity, achieving the highest overall AUC-ROC of 0.863 (95% CI: 0.725–0.967). Given the severe clinical consequences of misdiagnosing a mucinous neoplasm as standard appendicitis, model thresholds were clinically optimized to prioritize sensitivity. Under this sensitivity-first paradigm, SwinUNETR-LP achieved an exceptional Sensitivity of 0.944 and a Negative Predictive Value (NPV) of 0.933. Conversely, fully fine-tuned models such as Attention-SwinUNETR (AUC: 0.751) and SegFormer3D-MSCA (AUC: 0.608) exhibited diminished performance on the external cohort, indicative of overfitting on the high-dimensional spatial data.</p>
