<h2>3.4. Explainability and Spatial Attention Verification (Grad-CAM)</h2>
<p>To address the "black-box" nature of deep learning—a common barrier to clinical adoption—we generated 3D Gradient-weighted Class Activation Maps (Grad-CAM). This allowed us to verify that the models were utilizing true morphological indicators rather than relying on spurious background correlations (the "Clever Hans" effect). 
When mapped against ground-truth Regions of Interest (ROIs) delineated by expert radiologists, the SwinUNETR-LP architecture exhibited highly localized attention maps that precisely encapsulated the mucinous lesions (True Positives). Furthermore, in True Negative cases (standard appendicitis), the model successfully suppressed high-intensity activations, confirming that it accurately distinguishes neoplastic cystic changes from localized inflammatory edema. Comparative spatial analysis revealed that poorly performing models (e.g., SegFormer3D) frequently exhibited fragmented or peripherally scattered attention, structurally explaining their higher false-positive rates.</p>
<hr />
<h1>4. Discussion</h1>
<h2>4.1. The "Curse of Dimensionality" and the Necessity of Linear Probing</h2>
<p>A pivotal methodological finding of this study is the empirical superiority of the Linear Probing (LP) strategy over fully fine-tuned or attention-augmented architectures in the context of 3D medical imaging. 3D volumetric data (CT/MRI) possesses exceptionally high spatial dimensionality. When attempting to fully train architectures containing tens of millions of parameters on a clinically typical, modestly sized dataset (~160 patients), models succumb to the "curse of dimensionality" and memorize patient-specific noise (overfitting).</p>
<p>Our results vividly demonstrate this phenomenon: The SwinUNETR-LP model—where the pre-trained feature extraction backbone was strictly frozen and only a lightweight dense classification head was updated—drastically outperformed the fully fine-tuned Attention-SwinUNETR (AUC: 0.863 vs. 0.751). By freezing the backbone, we aggressively constrained the model's hypothesis space. This forced the network to rely exclusively on robust, generalized anatomical representations acquired during its initial pre-training, proving that parameter regularization via Linear Probing is not merely a computationally efficient alternative, but a functionally superior diagnostic strategy for small-cohort 3D radiomics.</p>
<h2>4.2. Clinical Implications of Sensitivity-First Thresholding</h2>
<p>From a clinical perspective, the cost of a false negative (discharging a patient with an occult mucinous neoplasm, risking pseudomyxoma peritonei) astronomically outweighs the cost of a false positive (unnecessary appendectomy for presumed standard appendicitis). Consequently, our pipeline was engineered with a strict sensitivity-first protocol utilizing Clinical Focal Loss. 
The efficacy of this framework is validated by the SwinUNETR-LP model’s NPV of 0.933 on the external test set. In clinical workflow terms, this exceptionally high NPV implies that when the AI predicts "standard appendicitis," the multidisciplinary tumor board can trust this negative prediction with high confidence, effectively streamlining triage and surgical planning.</p>
<h2>4.3. Independent External Validation as a Proxy for Generalizability</h2>
<p>A critical prerequisite for translating diagnostic AI into clinical practice is demonstrating robust generalizability. In this study, the models were evaluated on a strictly segregated external test cohort (N=37). By enforcing absolute patient-level separation and withholding this cohort during all phases of training, cross-validation, and hyperparameter tuning, we ensured an unbiased assessment of the algorithm’s real-world diagnostic utility. The preservation of high diagnostic metrics (AUC 0.863) on this independent cohort confirms that the pipeline does not suffer from single-cohort overfitting, satisfying the rigorous generalizability requirements mandated for multi-institutional clinical deployment.</p>
<hr />
